C7  - 2550479
DO  - 10.1142/S0218126625504791
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/inward/record.uri?eid=2-s2.0-105018890829&doi=10.1142%2FS0218126625504791&partnerID=40&md5=8a1919660282e693cca6a68a172d4de2
AB  - With the increasing competition for college graduates in the labor market, an innovative and efficient way to improve the employability and satisfaction of graduates is required. This research develops a College Student Employment Recommendation System powered by the Deep Neural Network (DNN) algorithm. The comprehensive dataset contains academic performance, skills and behavioral characteristics, which provide personalized job recommendations. Advanced data pre-processing methods guarantee the input data’s quality and consistency, with the critical features affecting the employability formed from the differential evolution algorithm (DEA). A DNN model for feature selection with an advanced neural network architecture enhances the accuracy of job performance prediction. The Teacher Needs Analysis framework is adapted to identify the training needs of educators, improving the relevance of live training evaluations via Kirkpatrick’s four-level Training Evaluation Model. Success in online learning is driven by interactive and adaptive modules that bridge the skill gap through individualized instruction and dynamic delivery of course content. Quality assurance promotes student-centered teaching, whereas an online student tracking system operates with a fuzzy logic model to retrospectively take the needed interventions as per the state of the student. Such a comprehensive approach ensures the alignment of student profiles to job requirements, further employability, and enhanced job satisfaction. The DNN-based recommendation system would provide the interface to leap from academic preparation to the industry’s expectations, improving confidence and adaptability during an increasingly competitive job market. The experimental results showed significant improvement in prediction accuracy, precision, recall and overall system performance, making it a robust solution for addressing employability challenges in the modern era. © 2025 Elsevier B.V., All rights reserved.
